idean set E×[0,1] is appendCoord(n) '' (E×I).\", \"depends_on\": [\"D001\", \"D008\", \"D009\"], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"m\", \"type\": \"ℕ\"}, {\"symbol\": \"x\", \"type\": \"Ambient m\"}, {\"symbol\": \"t\", \"type\": \"ℝ\"}], \"objects\": [\"m\", \"x\", \"t\", \"n\", \"E\", \"Ambient\", \"MaxProd\", \"I\"]}, {\"id\": \"D011\", \"symbol\": \"volume\", \"type\": \"measure on the Borel sets of ℝ\", \"definition\": \"volume is Lebesgue measure on ℝ, with its usual Borel measurable structure (and its extension as an outer measure on arbitrary sets). Thus measure monotonicity applies to all subsets; no measurability of a covering set is implicit.\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [], \"objects\": []}, {\"id\": \"D012\", \"symbol\": \"indicator\", \"type\": \"subset × constant → functions\", \"definition\": \"For a set B⊆Z and a scalar c∈[0,∞], c1_B denotes the function equal to c on B and zero off B, that is B.indicator(fun _ => c). This is meaningful for c=∞ as well, with no subtraction of infinities.\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"Z\", \"type\": \"type\"}, {\"symbol\": \"B\", \"type\": \"subset of Z\"}, {\"symbol\": \"c\", \"type\": \"[0,∞]\"}], \"objects\": [\"Z\", \"B\", \"c\"]}, {\"id\": \"D013\", \"symbol\": \"lintegral\", \"type\": \"measure × nonnegative extended function → [0,∞]\", \"definition\": \"For a measurable space Z, measure μ and f:Z→[0,∞], ∫⁻ f dμ (the lower Lebesgue integral, lintegral) is the supremum of ∫g dμ over measurable nonnegative simple functions g≤f; a simple function integral is its finite sum of values times the measures of the corresponding disjoint level sets. All integrals in the slicing proof are lower integrals; integrability is never required.\", \"depends_on\": [\"D012\"], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"Z\", \"type\": \"measurable space\"}, {\"symbol\": \"μ\", \"type\": \"measure on Z\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"Z→[0,∞]\"}, {\"symbol\": \"g\", \"type\": \"nonnegative measurable simple function on Z\"}], \"objects\": [\"Z\", \"μ\", \"f\", \"g\", \"indicator\"]}, {\"id\": \"D014\", \"symbol\": \"LipschitzWith\", \"type\": \"nonnegative real × map of pseudo extended metric spaces → propositions\", \"definition\": \"LipschitzWith(K,f) means ∀x,y, edist(f x,f y)≤K·edist(x,y), where K is a finite nonnegative real embedded into [0,∞]. On metric spaces this is the usual distance inequality.\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"K\", \"type\": \"nonnegative real\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"map between pseudo extended metric spaces\"}, {\"symbol\": \"x\", \"type\": \"point of source of f\"}, {\"symbol\": \"y\", \"type\": \"point of source of f\"}], \"objects\": [\"K\", \"f\", \"x\", \"y\"]}, {\"id\": \"D015\", \"symbol\": \"AntilipschitzWith\", \"type\": \"nonnegative real × map of extended metric spaces → propositions\", \"definition\": \"AntilipschitzWith(L,f) means ∀x,y, edist(x,y)≤L·edist(f x,f y), with finite L≥0. On metric spaces this is the corresponding ordinary distance inequality.\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"L\", \"type\": \"nonnegative real\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"map between extended metric spaces\"}, {\"symbol\": \"x\", \"type\": \"point of source of f\"}, {\"symbol\": \"y\", \"type\": \"point of source of f\"}], \"objects\": [\"L\", \"f\", \"x\", \"y\"]}, {\"id\": \"D016\", \"symbol\": \"segment\", \"type\": \"ordered field or real vector space × endpoints → subsets\", \"definition\": \"segment(k,x,y)={a•x+b•y: a,b∈k, a≥0,b≥0,a+b=1}, in a vector space over an ordered scalar field k; over ℝ this is the usual closed line segment.\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"k\", \"type\": \"ordered scalar field\"}, {\"symbol\": \"x\", \"type\": \"vector\"}, {\"symbol\": \"y\", \"type\": \"vector\"}, {\"symbol\": \"a\", \"type\": \"k\"}, {\"symbol\": \"b\", \"type\": \"k\"}], \"objects\": [\"k\", \"x\", \"y\", \"a\", \"b\"]}, {\"id\": \"D017\", \"symbol\": \"finrank\", \"type\": \"field × vector space → ℕ\", \"definition\": \"finrank(k,Z) is the dimension as a natural number of a finite-dimensional k-vector space Z (and is zero by the native total convention if the vector-space rank is infinite). In this proof its arguments are finite dimensional.\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"k\", \"type\": \"field\"}, {\"symbol\": \"Z\", \"type\": \"k-vector space\"}], \"objects\": [\"k\", \"Z\"]}, {\"id\": \"D018\", \"symbol\": \"rpow\", \"type\": \"real or extended nonnegative base × real exponent → corresponding scalars\", \"definition\": \"The notation v^u uses the standard real power; on positive real v it is exp(u log v), with 0^u=0 for u>0 and 0^0=1. For extended nonnegative bases it is the usual extension: at u>0, ∞^u=∞ and 0^u=0; at u=0 the value is 1; at u<0, 0^u=∞ and ∞^u=0. Only nonnegative real bases and positive exponents occur in the covering calculations. The native real function at negative v is defined using |v|, as in Real.rpow.\", \"depends_on\": [], \"requires\": [], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"v\", \"type\": \"real or extended nonnegative scalar\"}, {\"symbol\": \"u\", \"type\": \"ℝ\"}], \"objects\": [\"v\", \"u\"]}], \"logic\": {\"classical\": true, \"rules\": [\"direct\", \"forall_introduction\", \"implication_introduction\", \"exists_elimination\", \"case_elimination\", \"contradiction_classical\"], \"calculations\": [\"equality\", \"definitions\", \"finite_algebra\", \"finite_order\", \"finite_sums\"], \"primitives\": [{\"symbol\": \"0\", \"type\": \"typed numeral\", \"meaning\": \"Zero of the explicitly specified mathematical type.\"}, {\"symbol\": \"1\", \"type\": \"typed numeral\", \"meaning\": \"One of the explicitly specified mathematical type.\"}, {\"symbol\": \"2\", \"type\": \"typed numeral\", \"meaning\": \"Two of the explicitly specified mathematical type, including an exponent two.\"}, {\"symbol\": \"ℕ\", \"type\": \"number system\", \"meaning\": \"The natural numbers with their usual arithmetic.\"}, {\"symbol\": \"ℤ\", \"type\": \"number system\", \"meaning\": \"The integers with their usual arithmetic.\"}, {\"symbol\": \"ℚ\", \"type\": \"number system\", \"meaning\": \"The rational numbers with their usual ordered-field operations.\"}, {\"symbol\": \"ℝ\", \"type\": \"number system\", \"meaning\": \"The real numbers with their usual ordered-field structure.\"}, {\"symbol\": \"ℂ\", \"type\": \"number system\", \"meaning\": \"The complex numbers with their usual field structure.\"}, {\"symbol\": \"∅\", \"type\": \"typed set notation\", \"meaning\": \"The empty set in the explicitly specified ambient type.\"}, {\"symbol\": \"∞\", \"type\": \"typed order notation\", \"meaning\": \"The top element in an explicitly specified extended ordered codomain.\"}]}, \"inputs\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/basis-evidence-000.txt\", \"sha256\": \"56242fc597019e658c0e8792098ddb686d913156e0d831a14366d114cbf743f0\"}, {\"path\": \"inputs/basis-evidence-001.txt\", \"sha256\": \"798feb90fd8b7b67bb141e32cb98666353862d2fdab9cdbe4b7f73ff06a037d0\"}, {\"path\": \"inputs/basis-proof.md\", \"sha256\": \"69a949b6bd7009a369a9522306b61c477541a647320988c7d72406cece12eb07\"}, {\"path\": \"inputs/basis-registry.json\", \"sha256\": \"56d69d41aa07412c525e293b9d7d74cce5ca289266629db5cdc101a180bfd8e7\"}, {\"path\": \"inputs/problem.md\", \"sha256\": \"56242fc597019e658c0e8792098ddb686d913156e0d831a14366d114cbf743f0\"}, {\"path\": \"inputs/proof-context.json\", \"sha256\": \"a657af6b4db978ef80df90b12137d98daef6add470998934a41b0dc5a9103059\"}, {\"path\": \"inputs/target-selection.json\", \"sha256\": \"798feb90fd8b7b67bb141e32cb98666353862d2fdab9cdbe4b7f73ff06a037d0\"}], \"pins\": {\"lean_version\": \"4.32.2\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"mathlib_root\": \"/Users/syyui/formalization-tools/lean-mathlib-v4.32.2/mathlib\"}}, \"has_more\": f