{"id":"plan.json","result":"{\"id\": \"plan.json\", \"pointer\": \"\", \"view\": \"value\", \"value\": {\"version\": 2, \"contract_sha256\": \"3c16e7914968071838256e9a20b8899b8f0f120a2fff3167e904b4d7d2489fc4\", \"representation_notes\": \"Keep the exact prepared YuiBasis header as prefix. Ambient n is EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n): Fin n is the coordinate index type, and EuclideanSpace is PiLp 2, so 2 is the norm exponent, not a dimension parameter. Native X×ℝ has the maximum metric (Prod.dist_eq); set products are Set.prod (×ˢ). appendCoord uses Fin.lastCases to put t at Fin.last n and x_i at i.castSucc, with WithLp.toLp 2 selecting the target Euclidean norm. T012/T013 prove the actual metric conversion. H s A is the native unnormalized hausdorffMeasure on the canonical Borel sigma algebra of each metric space, with the BorelSpace equality witnessed definitionally; it adds no regularity assumption. C s δ A is exactly prepared Content s (ENNReal.ofReal δ) A. ENNReal.ofReal embeds max(δ,0), and only positive real scales occur in content assertions. Metric.ediam, infima, suprema, sums, dimensions and costs take values in ℝ≥0∞, retaining ∞; ^ on extended diameters is ENNReal real power, while ^ on real budgets is Real.rpow before ofReal. Positive exponents make empty costs zero. dimH uses Borel Hausdorff measure and nonnegative critical exponents intrinsically. Finite-dimensional and finite-product results imply that the final ENNReal equality is also equality of real dimensions. All H premises and N/P outputs are kept; no E exists here, no M port is needed, and reviewed background notes introduce no assumptions.\", \"steps\": [{\"id\": \"T001\", \"statement\": \"(0 ≤ dimH E ∧ dimH E ≤ (n : ℝ≥0∞)) ∧ dimH E ≠ ⊤\", \"inputs\": [], \"source_steps\": [\"S001\"], \"explanation\": \"N004: use dimension monotonicity into the whole finite-dimensional real EuclideanSpace and its finrank n. The empty coordinate family includes n=0; compactness and nonemptiness are unnecessary.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R004.BG001\"]}, {\"id\": \"T002\", \"statement\": \"dimH YuiBasis.unitInterval = 1\", \"inputs\": [], \"source_steps\": [\"S002\"], \"explanation\": \"N005: identify the real segment from 0 to 1 with Icc 0 1 and apply the native nondegenerate-segment dimension result.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R005.BG001\"]}, {\"id\": \"T003\", \"statement\": \"∀ (X : Type u) [MetricSpace X] (A : Set X) (s : ℝ), 0 < s →\\n  (∀ δ : ℝ, 0 < δ →\\n    C s δ A = ⨅ (U : ℕ → Set X) (_ : A ⊆ ⋃ i, U i)\\n      (_ : ∀ i, Metric.ediam (U i) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal δ), ∑' i, Metric.ediam (U i) ^ s) ∧\\n  (∀ …6427 tokens truncated…requires a conditionally complete lattice with compatible bornology.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"reason\": \"Each Bproj_i is contained in a finite interval in its nonempty branch.\"}], \"justification\": \"A real set lying in a ball of radius R about a lies in [a-R,a+R]. Conversely a set contained between real bounds p,q has distances from p bounded by max(q-p,0). The usual real order is conditionally complete. These facts identify the order and metric boundedness structures used in the stated application.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG003\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/2/text\", \"fact\": \"For arbitrary finite real endpoints a,b the interval [a,b] is closed, hence Borel measurable, also when empty. The empty set and finite intersections of such sets are Borel measurable.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/2/text\", \"reason\": \"J_i is either empty or a closed real interval; I=[0,1].\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/5\", \"reason\": \"D008 defines I.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/7\", \"reason\": \"D011 fixes the Borel measurable structure on R.\"}], \"justification\": \"The complement of [a,b] is the union of the open rays (-infinity,a) and (b,infinity). Closed sets are complements of open sets and belong to the Borel sigma algebra, which is closed under finite intersections.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG004\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/3/text\", \"fact\": \"For u>0, the map d -> d^u is increasing on extended nonnegative reals. For finite r>=0, (ofReal(r))^u=ofReal(r^u); also (ofReal(r^u))*ofReal(r)=ofReal(r^(u+1)).\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/produces/0\", \"reason\": \"F001 gives finite 0<=rho_i<=delta and the projection diameter bounds.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"reason\": \"L1 gives s>0, hence s+1>0.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/11\", \"reason\": \"D018 supplies the standard power conventions.\"}], \"justification\": \"On positive finite bases, monotonicity follows from increasing log and exp and multiplication by u>0. The identity r^u*r=r^(u+1) follows by adding exponents in exp(u log r). At r=0 both positive powers vanish, and the extended endpoints follow from 0^u=0 and infinity^u=infinity. Embedding finite nonnegative reals preserves products and the defined powers. These facts justify both the slice-cost comparison here and the termwise power identity in S005.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG005\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/3/text\", \"fact\": \"For any measurable set B in a measurable space and fixed k in [0,infinity], the function k1_B with values in [0,infinity] is measurable, including k=infinity. A measurable function is almost-everywhere measurable for every measure.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/2/produces/0\", \"reason\": \"F003 supplies measurability of I and every J_i intersect I.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/3/text\", \"reason\": \"h and g_i are exactly constant indicators on those sets.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/8\", \"reason\": \"D012 defines an indicator by its values, including an infinite constant.\"}], \"justification\": \"The inverse image of a Borel set under k1_B is one of the empty set, B, its complement, or the entire space, depending on membership of 0 and k. All are measurable. Almost-everywhere measurability then holds using the function itself as the measurable representative, with no exceptional set.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG006\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/4/text\", \"fact\": \"Multiplication by a fixed nonnegative extended scalar preserves inequalities (with 0*infinity=0). For sequences a_i<=b_i in [0,infinity], sum_i a_i<=sum_i b_i, without finiteness assumptions.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/2/produces/0\", \"reason\": \"F003 bounds volume(J_i) by ofReal(rho_i).\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/4/text\", \"reason\": \"The displayed chain multiplies measure inequalities by nonnegative ofReal(rho_i^s) and then sums.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/7\", \"reason\": \"D011 supplies measure monotonicity on J_i intersect I subset J_i.\"}], \"justification\": \"For finite nonnegative scalars multiplication is order preserving; the zero and infinity cases satisfy the same inequality directly under the stated conventions. Compare each finite partial sum termwise, then take the supremum over finite index sets, which defines the nonnegative extended sum.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG007\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/5/text\", \"fact\": \"In [0,infinity], if c<=k for every member k of a family K, then c<=inf K, including K empty with inf K=infinity. If f(delta)<=g(delta) for every delta>0, then sup_{delta>0} f(delta)<=sup_{delta>0} g(delta), including infinite suprema.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/4/produces/0\", \"reason\": \"F005 makes C_s(delta,A) a lower bound for every admissible product-cover cost.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inputs/0/statement\", \"reason\": \"N101 identifies these infima with the positive-exponent contents and identifies the scale suprema with H.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/5/text\", \"reason\": \"The scale inequality has been proved for arbitrary positive delta and s+1 is positive.\"}], \"justification\": \"An infimum is the greatest lower bound, so every lower bound is below it; for an empty family this says only c<=infinity. Each f(delta) is below the supremum of g, making that supremum an upper bound for all f(delta); the least-upper-bound property yields the stated supremum inequality. No interchange of infimum, supremum or integral is involved.\"}], \"R103\": [{\"id\": \"BG001\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"fact\": \"For 0<a≤1, the natural ceiling N=ceil(1/a) is finite and satisfies N≥1, 1/N≤a and N≤1/a+1≤2/a. The N closed intervals [j/N,(j+1)/N], 0≤j<N, cover [0,1] and have diameter 1/N.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/produces/0\", \"reason\": \"F001 supplies 0<a_i≤r for the chosen budgets.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"reason\": \"r=min(1,δ), with δ>0, gives r≤1.\"}], \"justification\": \"The Archimedean property of the reals gives the least natural integer N≥1/a; its minimality gives N<1/a+1. Positivity permits reciprocation to obtain 1/N≤a. Adjacent interval endpoints agree, their first endpoint is zero and last is one, so their finite union is [0,1]. Distances in each interval are at most its endpoint difference, attained by its endpoints.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG002\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"fact\": \"If ediam U≤ofReal a with finite a≥0 and J is a real interval of length b≤a, then ediam(U×J)≤ofReal a in MaxProd X. This also holds when U is empty.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/produces/0\", \"reason\": \"The selected pair supplies ediam U_i≤ofReal a_i and a_i>0.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"reason\": \"The constructed intervals have length 1/N_i≤a_i.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/0\", \"reason\": \"Extended diameter is the supremum of pairwise extended distances.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/6\", \"reason\": \"The product distance is the maximum of the two component distances.\"}], \"justification\": \"Every pair from U has distance ≤a by the diameter bound and the order-preserving embedding of nonnegative finite reals. Every pair from J has distance ≤b≤a. Hence each product distance, their maximum, is ≤a, and taking its supremum preserves the upper bound. An empty product has diameter zero.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG003\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"fact\": \"For p>0 the extended nonnegative power x↦x^p is increasing. For finite a>0, (ofReal a)^p=ofReal(a^p), and for q>0 one has a^(q+1)=a*a^q. Consequently, N finite product sets each of diameter ≤ofReal a have total p-cost ≤ofReal(N*a^p), including empty sets when p>0.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"reason\": \"L1 gives q>0; thus p=q+1>0.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/produces/0\", \"reason\": \"The chosen budgets are strictly positive finite reals.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"reason\": \"The product diameter bounds and finite positive integers N_i have been established.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/7\", \"reason\": \"Real powers use exp(p log a) on positive bases and zero at base zero for positive exponents.\"}], \"justification\": \"Monotonicity of log and exp proves power monotonicity for positive finite bases; the specified zero and infinity values give the endpoints. The exponential addition law gives a^(q+1)=a*a^q. Embedding nonnegative finite values commutes with these powers and finite sums. Bounding each of N costs and summing gives the stated inequality; empty-set cost is zero.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG004\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"fact\": \"For finite natural N_i≥1, the set T={(i,j):i∈ℕ,j<N_i} has a natural enumeration without repetitions. For nonnegative extended costs c_(i,j), enumeration preserves the total sum and Σ_T c=Σ_i Σ_(j<N_i)c_(i,j). Nonnegative sums preserve termwise inequalities and commute with multiplication by a finite positive constant, in particular 2.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"reason\": \"Each N_i is a finite positive natural; all product costs and comparison terms lie in [0,∞].\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/2\", \"reason\": \"Cover costs are nonnegative extended sums over natural indices.\"}], \"justification\": \"List pairs in successive finite diagonals of ℕ×ℕ and retain exactly those in T. Every admissible pair occurs once; the pairs (i,0) show that the retained list is infinite. A nonnegative sum is the supremum of its finite subsums, so a bijection preserves it. Every finite set of pairs is contained in finitely many complete finite rows; conversely such rows form a finite set of pairs. This gives the regrouping identity. Termwise comparison follows on finite subsums. Multiplication by a fixed positive finite constant is an order isomorphism of [0,∞], hence preserves their suprema and gives the scaling identity.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG005\", \"kind\": \"elementary\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/2/text\", \"fact\": \"If z∈[0,∞] satisfies z≤ofReal(2ε) for every real ε>0, then z=0.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/1/produces/0\", \"reason\": \"F002 supplies a cover of cost <ofReal(2ε) for every positive ε at the fixed positive scale.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inputs/0/statement\", \"reason\": \"N101 identifies the relevant content with the infimum of these cover costs.\"}], \"justification\": \"Choosing ε=1 bounds z by the finite value 2, excluding infinity. If its finite real value t were positive, ε=t/4 would give t≤t/2, a contradiction. Nonnegativity therefore forces z=0. The subsequent supremum is a supremum of zero contents by N101, without any exchange of limits or integrals.\"}], \"R104\": [{\"id\": \"BG001\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"fact\": \"For a metric space X, d((x,t),(y,u))=max(d_X(x,y),|t−u|) is a finite metric on X×ℝ. The usual real metric and this product metric induce extended metrics, so the supplied extended-metric dimension statements apply to them.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/locals/0\", \"reason\": \"X is an arbitrary metric space.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/3\", \"reason\": \"D009 specifies exactly this product set and distance; the real factor has its usual metric.\"}], \"justification\": \"Nonnegativity and symmetry follow from those of each factor. The maximum vanishes exactly when both factor distances vanish, hence exactly when both coordinates coincide. For three points the two factor triangle inequalities and max(a+c,b+d)≤max(a,b)+max(c,d) prove the product triangle inequality. All distances are finite. Embedding a finite nonnegative distance into [0,∞] preserves zero, order and addition, yielding the corresponding extended metric axioms.\"}], \"R105\": [{\"id\": \"BG001\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"fact\": \"For z∈[0,∞] with z≠∞, z.toReal≥0 and ofReal(z.toReal)=z. For nonnegative real a,b, ofReal a≤ofReal b iff a≤b, likewise for strict inequality, and ofReal(a+b)=ofReal a+ofReal b. Every ofReal b is finite; consequently z≤ofReal b implies z≠∞. Also 0≠∞ and ∞≤z implies z=∞.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/definitions/1\", \"reason\": \"D003 defines ofReal as the nonnegative real embedding and toReal as the finite real value, with infinity the added top element.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/6\", \"reason\": \"D007 places dimH in [0,∞].\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"reason\": \"L1 provides dimH A≠∞, the actual condition for recovering dimH A from D0.\"}], \"justification\": \"The extended nonnegative reals consist of the nonnegative reals and an added largest element infinity. On finite nonnegative values, the embedding and finite-value map are inverse and the order and addition are the real ones. A finite embedded value is strictly below infinity, so a value bounded by it cannot be infinity. Top-element antisymmetry gives the last implication. These facts justify the initial identification and subsequent finite-value comparisons and addition; S004 establishes product finiteness before applying the finite inverse there.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG002\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/2/text\", \"fact\": \"For any metric space X, the formula d((x,t),(y,u))=max(d_X(x,y),|t−u|) makes X×ℝ a metric space and hence an extended metric space via edist=ofReal d. Equipping it with its own Borel sigma algebra satisfies the Borel-space premises of M004 and M005 without any assumption on the subset G.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/locals/0\", \"reason\": \"X is an arbitrary metric space with its Borel sigma algebra.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/8\", \"reason\": \"D009 specifies this maximum distance and chooses the Borel sigma algebra for Hausdorff-measure statements.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/0\", \"reason\": \"D002 identifies metric edist with ofReal of distance.\"}], \"justification\": \"Nonnegativity and symmetry hold in each coordinate; distance zero forces both coordinates equal. Each coordinate distance obeys the triangle inequality and is bounded by the maximum on its corresponding leg, so both coordinate distances across a triangle are bounded by the sum of the two leg maxima; taking their maximum proves the product triangle inequality. Embedding the nonnegative metric distances gives the extended metric inequalities. The sigma algebra generated by this metric's open sets is its Borel structure by construction; no product-sigma-algebra identification or measurability of G is needed.\"}], \"R106\": [{\"id\": \"BG001\", \"kind\": \"elementary\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"fact\": \"For a,b,d≥0 with d²=a²+b² and m=max(a,b), one has m≤d≤√2·m. Here √2 is finite nonnegative and (√2)²=2.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"reason\": \"a and b are respectively a Euclidean norm and an absolute value; d is the Euclidean target distance, and the displayed finite-sum computation establishes d²=a²+b².\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/0\", \"reason\": \"The norm is the nonnegative square root of the finite sum of coordinate squares.\"}], \"justification\": \"Since 0≤a,b≤m and m is one of a,b, m²≤a²+b²≤2m²=(√2·m)². Squaring preserves and reflects order between nonnegative reals: if 0≤x<y then y²−x²=(y−x)(y+x)>0. Apply this to the two inequalities. These are exactly the square-root and sign conditions used in S001.\"}, {\"id\": \"BG002\", \"kind\": \"mathematical\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"fact\": \"If (Z,d) is a metric space, then d_max((x,t),(y,u))=max(d(x,y),|t−u|) is a metric on Z×ℝ; its finite distances canonically define an extended metric. Thus MaxProd(Ambient n) and Ambient(n+1) satisfy the space hypotheses of M006/M007 for every n≥0.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/definitions/0\", \"reason\": \"Ambient m is the specified Euclidean inner product space with its norm distance, for all natural m including zero.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/4\", \"reason\": \"MaxProd uses exactly the maximum of the given metric distance and real absolute difference.\"}], \"justification\": \"Nonnegativity and symmetry follow for each component. A zero maximum forces both component distances to vanish, hence equality of both coordinates. Each component distance across three points is at most the sum of the corresponding two component distances, which is at most the sum of the two maxima; taking the maximum proves the triangle inequality. Embedding nonnegative finite distances into [0,∞] preserves these metric axioms. No dimension or nonemptiness condition is introduced.\"}], \"R107\": [{\"id\": \"BG001\", \"kind\": \"elementary\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"fact\": \"For finite a,b∈[0,∞], if b=a+1 then b.toReal=a.toReal+1, and a+1 is finite. Here a=dimH(E) and b=dimH(appendCoord(n) '' (E×I)).\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/definitions/1\", \"reason\": \"D003 identifies finite extended nonnegative reals with their real values.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inputs/1\", \"reason\": \"N004 supplies finiteness of dimH(E).\"}, {\"location\": \"/inputs/2\", \"reason\": \"N105 supplies finiteness and the dimension equality for the maximum product.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inputs/3\", \"reason\": \"N106 transfers that product dimension, hence its finiteness and equality, to the Euclidean image.\"}], \"justification\": \"Finite extended nonnegative reals are nonnegative real numbers under the canonical embedding. Their finite sums agree with real addition; the sum of the finite representatives a.toReal and 1 is finite. Applying the inverse real-value identification to b=a+1 gives the stated equality.\"}]}, \"sha256\": \"7939fb16e2685fee4b2216e966e5582d0bf36b50e4e297fd9169c517d60112c8\"}, \"has_more\": false, \"next_offset\": null}"}
